    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d717">

                <pb facs="0564" n="556" sameAs="frus:frus1937v04/pg556"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/13931: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-01-19T10:00:00+08:00">January 19, 1937—10
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received January 19—1 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>32. A telegram filed by Paxton at Loyang on January 18, 11 p.m. states: <list>
                        <label>1.</label>
                        <item>That Barrett and Wang<anchor n="14" xml:id="d717-f1"/> returned from
                            Sian to Loyang without foreign passengers.</item>
                        <label>2.</label>
                        <item>That 75 foreigners had already left Sian with Scott and had proceeded
                            to Tungkwan.</item>
                        <label>3.</label>
                        <item>That Scott had returned to Sian after having carried out the
                            evacuation.</item>
                        <label>4.</label>
                        <item>That the foregoing information had been confirmed by telephone call
                            from Paxton to Swenson at Tungkwan.</item>
                        <label>5.</label>
                        <item>That Barrett and Fraser were returning to Sian to evacuate Scott and
                            perhaps five British and three Russian[s] who still remained
                            there.</item>
                    </list></p>
                <p>The evacuation from Sian was presumably effected by bus in accordance with a plan
                    arranged by Peck and Captain Scott with Sian authorities during Peck’s visit
                    there.</p>
                <p>Sent to the Department, Peiping, Hankow, Shanghai.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d717-f1" n="14" xml:id="d717-f1-content">
                <p>Wang Kwong, of the Chinese Foreign Office.</p>
            </note>
